      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.024,
            0.806,
            0.048
          ],
          "content": "-000 Chapter 5 — Exploring Forces"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.089,
            0.259,
            0.111
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Snapshots"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.126,
            0.813,
            0.16
          ],
          "content": "A force is push or pull on an object resulting from the object’s interaction with another object."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.16,
            0.617,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": "The SI unit of force is newton and its symbol is N."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.177,
            0.525,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "Forces can act with or without contact."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.781,
            0.009,
            0.939,
            0.203
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.192,
            0.814,
            0.225
          ],
          "content": "Muscular force and frictional force are some of the examples of contact forces."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.225,
            0.814,
            0.258
          ],
          "content": "Magnetic force, gravitational force, and electrostatic force are non-contact forces."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.258,
            0.813,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "Force can change an object’s speed, direction of its motion, or both. Force can also change the shape of an object."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.292,
            0.814,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": "The force which comes into play when an object moves or tries to move over another surface, is called force of friction or simply friction. It acts in a direction opposite to the direction in which the object is moving or trying to move."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.356,
            0.815,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "The force exerted by a magnet on another magnet or a magnetic material is called magnetic force."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.389,
            0.814,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": "The force exerted by a charged body on another charged body or uncharged body is called an electrostatic force."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.421,
            0.814,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": "The force with which the Earth attracts objects towards itself, is called the gravitational force. It is always an attractive force."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.454,
            0.814,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": "The force with which the Earth pulls an object towards itself is called the weight of the object. The SI unit of weight is newton (N)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.487,
            0.814,
            0.52
          ],
          "content": "The mass of an object remains unchanged whereas its weight may vary from place to place."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.519,
            0.813,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": "When an object is placed in a liquid, the force applied by a liquid on an object in the upward direction is known as upthrust or buoyant force."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.565,
            0.423,
            0.588
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Keep the curiosity alive"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.604,
            0.679,
            0.624
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "1. Match items in Column A with the items in Column B."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.628,
            0.792,
            0.81
          ],
          "content": "| Column A (Type of force) | Column B (Example) |\n| --- | --- |\n| (i) Muscular force | (a) A cricket ball stopping on its own just before touching the boundary line |\n| (ii) Magnetic force | (b) A child lifting a school bag |\n| (iii) Frictional force | (c) A fruit falling from a tree |\n| (iv) Gravitational force | (d) Balloon rubbed on woollen cloth attracting hair strands |\n| (v) Electrostatic force | (e) A compass needle pointing North |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_table_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
